  "query_preview": "..\n\nNow located off the coast of northern Baja, Tropical Storm\nRachel continues its northeastward track. While TS Rachel is not\nforecast to make landfall until tonight, ahead of the system\ndeep moisture and instability is surging into the southwest US\nwith the aid of a deep trough off the coast of southern \nCalifornia. This trough reinforcing the tropical system is\ntightening the pressure gradient leading to strong to gusty\nwinds across the region. South to southwest winds will begin to\nincrease overnight Saturday peaking Sunday afternoon. The extent\nof strong winds will push as far east as Tucson but breezy winds\nwill extend into Cochise and Graham counties. The Wind Advisory\ncontinues to be on track for areas from Tucson westward for\nsouth to southwest winds sustained at 25 to 35 mph gusting 45 \nto 55 mph. \n\nIn regards to todays impacts from TS Rachel, isolated to \nscattered light showers have expanded across southern Arizona \nwith some embedded thunderstorms. Thunderstorms continue to have\nthe potential to become severe with the main threats being \ndamaging wind gusts(40\u201360 mph), small hail, and localized heavy \ndownpours. There is still a non-zero threat for tornadoes in \nfar western Pima county (including Ajo, Lukeville, and Why) to \nthe combination of modest instability, low-level helicity and \nstrong deep layer shear. Greatest chance for severe \nthunderstorms will start this afternoon and potentially go into \nthe overnight hours. \n\nThe concern for flash flooding remains low, with only isolated\nflash flooding expected. Storms will become scattered to \nnumerous this afternoon into the late evening with storm total \nrainfall around 0.25\" to 0.75\" for areas west of Tucson and 0.1\"\nto 0.25\" for areas east of Tucson. Mountain areas could see \nrain totals between 1\" to 2\". Flash flooding may be possible for\nareas that are underneath storms that train or are stronger. \n\nOnce TS Rachel become post tropical Sunday, southerly flow \nahead of the longwave trough will linger keeping chances for\nrain and thunderstorms 20-50% for Monday and Tuesday. Drier air\nreturns by Wednesday, clearing out shower activity across the \narea. Expect an extended period of below- normal temperatures \nstarting Sunday and persisting through mid- week.",
